---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const enzymeCfg = {
  curves: [{ expr: 'vmax*x/(x + km)', label: 'reaction rate (Michaelis–Menten)', color: '#2e7d32', width: 2.4 }],
  params: [
    { name: 'vmax', label: 'max rate Vmax', min: 20, max: 100, step: 5, value: 60 },
    { name: 'km', label: 'Km (substrate at half-max)', min: 20, max: 200, step: 10, value: 80 },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 400, label: 'substrate concentration' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 100, label: 'rate' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 340,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/intermediate/enzymes-and-metabolism">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Life's chemistry runs at 37 °C — far too cool for most reactions. The solution is
    <strong>enzymes: protein catalysts that grip specific molecules and strain them toward
    reaction</strong>, accelerating rates by factors of millions. Metabolism is the whole network
    of these reactions: thousands of steps, all regulated, all balancing.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the Michaelis–Menten curve — fast at first, then saturated; drag Vmax and Km like a mutation would" widget="plotter" config={enzymeCfg} />

  <p>
    At low substrate, rate climbs almost linearly (empty enzymes grabbing whatever passes); at
    high substrate every enzyme works flat out — the plateau at Vmax. <strong>Km</strong> is the
    substrate concentration at half-max: a measure of grip strength. A mutation raising Km makes
    the enzyme worse at its job — the molecular basis of many genetic diseases and of drug
    design (inhibitors are just Km-wreckers).
  </p>

  <h2>How metabolism is organised</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pathways:</strong> reactions chained end to end — glycolysis, the Krebs cycle — each enzyme a station on the assembly line.</li>
    <li><strong>Regulation is layered:</strong> feedback inhibition (the product switches off the first enzyme) acts in seconds; gene expression changes the enzyme supply in hours. Cells tune instantly and long-term.</li>
    <li><strong>Coupling:</strong> unfavourable reactions ride on favourable ones via ATP. Life never gets free energy — it channels it (the photosynthesis lesson shows the source).</li>
    <li><strong>Metabolic drugs:</strong> statins block an enzyme in cholesterol synthesis; aspirin blocks another in inflammation. Pharmacology is largely enzyme interference with a spec sheet.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Michaelis & Menten (1913); Berg, Tymoczko & Stryer, Biochemistry; OpenStax, Biology 2e (Metabolism).</p>
</TopicPage>
